# Validation Report: BB-FD-S0014-001

Finding title: Libvirt VM egress policy can apply without fail-closed MAC and IPv6 guards

Candidate id: BB-FD-S0014-001
Instance key: egress-bypass:src/blastbox/host/runtime/libvirt_egress.py:324
Ledger row id: candidate_ledger.jsonl:1
Root-control file:line: src/blastbox/host/runtime/libvirt_egress.py:320
Affected locations: src/blastbox/host/runtime/libvirt_vm.py:217, src/blastbox/host/runtime/libvirt_vm.py:239, src/blastbox/host/runtime/libvirt_egress.py:320, src/blastbox/host/runtime/libvirt_egress.py:324, src/blastbox/host/runtime/libvirt_egress.py:335, src/blastbox/host/runtime/libvirt_egress.py:338
Advisory/source reference: raw_candidates.jsonl:1 from file-review-shard-0014
Seed anchor: none provided beyond the raw candidate locations

Disposition: reportable
Survives: yes
Confidence: medium (0.68)
Validation method: bounded static trace plus targeted monkeypatch/unit-style proof of `LibvirtEgress.apply()` behavior without libvirt or privileged iptables.

## Rubric Checklist

- [x] Reachability: `LibvirtEgress.apply()` can be called with `mac=None` and applies a policy when the slot already has a worker IP; however, a fresh `spawn()` path with `_domain_mac()==None` does not discover an IP and does not reach `apply()`.
- [x] IPv4 MAC binding: with `mac=None`, `apply()` installs the source-IP FORWARD jump but no MAC anti-spoof rule or fail-closed rejection.
- [x] IPv6 fail-closed behavior: with a MAC present, `apply()` invokes `ip6tables` without `check=True`; a failed IPv6 guard does not raise or roll back.
- [x] Security boundary and preconditions: the path is the untrusted libvirt VM worker egress boundary described by the threat model.
- [ ] Deployment proof: no local libvirt network configuration or live VM proof establishes whether the deployed VM network is IPv4-only, IPv6-routed, or permissive to re-IPed guest traffic.

## Source, Control, Sink

Source: malicious code running inside a libvirt VM worker after a job is handed to the guest. The relevant attacker actions are changing the guest source IPv4 address after policy application or emitting IPv6 traffic if the libvirt network provides IPv6.

Closest control: `LibvirtEgress.apply()` creates the per-worker IPv4 FORWARD chain and inserts `FORWARD -s <worker_ip> -j <chain>` at src/blastbox/host/runtime/libvirt_egress.py:320. The MAC anti-spoof control is conditional at lines 324-326. The IPv6 drop is conditional on `mac` and unchecked at lines 335-338.

Sink: host forwarding/routing policy for the VM worker. Packets that do not match the original IPv4 source jump, or IPv6 packets when the ip6tables guard is absent/failed, can bypass the intended egress personality controls.

Preconditions: libvirt VM runtime is used with `egress_policy` configured; the VM guest can alter network behavior after compromise or malicious sample execution; for the MAC-none IPv4 branch, a caller or slot state must provide a worker IP while `mac` is `None`; for the IPv6 branch, the libvirt/host network must have an IPv6 route or forwarding path and `ip6tables` must be unavailable or fail.

Counterevidence: `LibvirtVmRuntime._domain_mac()` can return `None` at src/blastbox/host/runtime/libvirt_vm.py:416-421, but the normal fresh-spawn readiness path calls `_ip_for_mac(slot.mac)` at line 229, and `_ip_for_mac(None)` returns `None` at lines 435-437. In that fresh no-MAC/no-IP path, `is_ready()` returns `False` before applying egress. The code comment at src/blastbox/host/runtime/libvirt_egress.py:334 also states worker networks are intended to be IPv4-only, but this is not enforced in code.

Proof gaps: no privileged live libvirt/iptables run was attempted; no repository deployment file proves the active libvirt network is IPv4-only; no packet-level proof shows re-IPed or IPv6 guest traffic leaving a real host. These are deployment proof gaps rather than exact countercontrols, because `apply()` itself does not fail closed on the tested conditions.

## Evidence Observed

- Static trace: `slot.mac = self._domain_mac(name)` at src/blastbox/host/runtime/libvirt_vm.py:217 may produce `None`; `is_ready()` passes `mac=slot.mac` into `LibvirtEgress.apply()` at lines 239-240 when a slot has an IP.
- Static trace: `LibvirtEgress.apply()` installs only an IPv4 source-IP jump at src/blastbox/host/runtime/libvirt_egress.py:320 before conditionally adding the MAC anti-spoof rule at lines 324-326.
- Static trace: the IPv6 guard at src/blastbox/host/runtime/libvirt_egress.py:338 calls `_priv([...])` without `check=True`, so a nonzero `ip6tables` return code is ignored.
- Targeted PoC: `validation_artifacts/poc_libvirt_egress_apply.py` monkeypatches the privileged runner. The generated log shows `mac=None` applies successfully with a source-IP FORWARD jump and no MAC or IPv6 guard, and a forced `ip6tables` failure still lets `apply()` return success.
- Caller proof: the same PoC shows a no-IP/no-MAC `VmSlot` does not call egress application, while a known-IP/no-MAC `VmSlot` calls `apply(..., mac=None)`.
- Sibling control: tests/host/test_netd.py:687-699 treats failed `ip6tables` plus reachable IPv6 as fail-closed for container egress, showing this VM path is weaker than the nearby intended pattern.

## Tested

Ran:

```sh
python3 /tmp/codex-security-scans/blastbox/c7e9f28495e1_20260626T144948Z/artifacts/05_findings/BB-FD-S0014-001/validation_artifacts/poc_libvirt_egress_apply.py > /tmp/codex-security-scans/blastbox/c7e9f28495e1_20260626T144948Z/artifacts/05_findings/BB-FD-S0014-001/validation_artifacts/poc_libvirt_egress_apply.log
```

The first attempt with `python` failed because this environment has `python3` but no `python`.

## Remaining Uncertainty

The IPv4 re-IP impact is weaker than the raw candidate stated for the fresh spawn path, because `_domain_mac()==None` also prevents IP discovery in the normal runtime flow. It remains relevant for `LibvirtEgress.apply()` and for any known-IP slot/caller path. The IPv6 fail-open behavior is directly reachable with normal MAC discovery, but exploitability depends on the deployed libvirt network having IPv6 forwarding or route availability.

Minimal next step: on a libvirt host, run an integration test with an IPv6-capable worker network and forced `ip6tables` failure, then confirm `is_ready()` finalizes the VM and guest IPv6 traffic can traverse or is otherwise absent by route proof.

