==== BB-FD-S0006-001 report head ====
# Validation Report: UrlGrab follows redirects to non-HTTP schemes and can seal worker-local files as artifacts

## Required Fields

- Title: UrlGrab follows redirects to non-HTTP schemes and can seal worker-local files as artifacts
- Candidate id: BB-FD-S0006-001
- Instance key: ssrf-file-read:src/blastbox/engines/urlgrab.py:135
- Root-control file:line: src/blastbox/engines/urlgrab.py:93
- Confidence: high (0.86)
- Method: bounded local targeted API reproduction without sockets, plus static source/control/sink trace

## Rubric Checklist

- [x] Attacker-controlled source: UrlGrab accepts a URL from job input; a redirecting HTTP(S) server controls the `Location` value.
- [x] Redirect behavior: `_CappedRedirect` inherits urllib redirect handling that creates a new request for the redirected URL.
- [x] Scheme control: `_is_valid_url` rejects direct `file:` input but is only applied before fetch; redirected `file:` was accepted by the redirect handler.
- [x] Artifact sink: the redirected body reached `UrlGrabEngine.detonate`, which wrote `body.bin` and declared artifact id `body`; the harness sealed it into `metadata.json`.
- [x] Realistic reproduction or exact static trace: the no-network PoC used the actual `_CappedRedirect`, default urllib opener handlers, `UrlGrabEngine`, and worker harness path. A live HTTP 30x hop was not exercised.

## Source, Control, Sink, Preconditions

- Source: a remote job submitter controls the first non-empty line read by `_read_url` at `src/blastbox/engines/urlgrab.py:79`; if it points to an attacker-controlled HTTP(S) endpoint, that endpoint controls the redirect `Location`.
- Closest control: `_is_valid_url` at `src/blastbox/engines/urlgrab.py:93` only allows the initial URL to be `http` or `https`; `_CappedRedirect` at `src/blastbox/engines/urlgrab.py:101` caps redirect count but does not restrict schemes.
- Sink: `_default_fetch` builds the default urllib opener at `src/blastbox/engines/urlgrab.py:135`, opens the request at `src/blastbox/engines/urlgrab.py:138`, and reads bytes at `src/blastbox/engines/urlgrab.py:144`; `UrlGrabEngine.detonate` writes those bytes to `body.bin` at `src/blastbox/engines/urlgrab.py:209` and declares artifact id `body` at `src/blastbox/engines/urlgrab.py:228`.
- Impact exposure: `run_detonation` seals declared artifacts from disk at `src/blastbox/worker/harness.py:205` and writes `metadata.json` at `src/blastbox/worker/harness.py:240`; the API artifact route serves artifacts by id at `src/blastbox/host/ingress/app.py:693`, with test coverage showing by-id artifact bytes are returned at `tests/host/ingress/test_app.py:543`.
- Preconditions: `UrlGrabEngine` is configured/allowed for the submitter, the worker can reach an attacker-controlled initial HTTP(S) URL, and the final worker-local file is readable by the worker and fits within `limits.max_artifact_bytes`.

## Evidence Observed

- Static code: the module states it performs one bounded HTTP GET and records final URL after redirects, but the only scheme validator is `_is_valid_url` before fetch (`src/blastbox/engines/urlgrab.py:93` to `src/blastbox/engines/urlgrab.py:99`).
- Static code: `_default_fetch` calls `urllib.request.build_opener(*handlers)` with `_CappedRedirect` and does not remove default `FileHandler`, `DataHandler`, or `FTPHandler` (`src/blastbox/engines/urlgrab.py:115` to `src/blastbox/engines/urlgrab.py:138`).
- Runtime proof: `poc_redirect_file_artifact.py` showed Python 3.12.3 `HTTPRedirectHandler.redirect_request` returned a request with `redirected_url` equal to `file:///tmp/.../worker-local-secret.txt`.
- Runtime proof: the default opener contained `FileHandler`, `DataHandler`, and `FTPHandler`; `opener.open(redirected).read()` returned the sentinel worker-local file body with SHA-256 `ceed24fdd0e6d58cff53859c15600a912bf576838746a0a4e4f4dd9a26ceb05c`.
- Runtime proof: `run_detonation(UrlGrabEngine(fetch_fn=...))` returned `0`; `metadata_status` was `ok`; `metadata_final_url` was the `file:` URL; metadata contained artifact id `body`, path `body.bin`, bytes `38`, and the same SHA-256; `body_matches_redirect_read` was `true`.
- Boundary evidence: README describes `urlgrab` as a reference example only, but repository docs also record a dispatcher-integrated batch where `urlgrab` was registered in `BLASTBOX_ENGINES` and allowed through the API (`docs/superpowers/specs/2026-06-17-sandboxing-landscape-and-snapshot-tier.md:91` to `:97`).

## Tested Steps

1. Created a sentinel file under this candidate's `validation_artifacts/` directory.
2. Built an initial `http://attacker.invalid/redirect` request and passed a `file:` redirect target into the actual `_CappedRedirect.redirect_request`.
3. Built `urllib.request.build_opener(_CappedRedirect())` and confirmed the opener includes non-HTTP handlers, including `FileHandler`.
4. Opened the redirected `file:` request with that opener and read the sentinel bytes.
5. Ran the real `UrlGrabEngine` and worker harness with the fetched bytes to verify `body.bin` and the sealed artifact metadata are produced.

## Counterevidence

- Direct `file:` job input is rejected by `_is_valid_url`; the issue requires an allowed initial `http` or `https` URL and a redirect.
- The claim is worker-local file exposure, not host filesystem read through the host trust boundary; host artifact path containment still prevents serving undeclared or traversal paths from the output directory.
- The README labels `urlgrab` as a reference example, reducing default production exposure, although documentation shows it has been registered and exercised through the API.
- Netpolicy can prevent the initial network hop if egress is disabled or the redirecting endpoint is unreachable.

## Proof Gaps

- The PoC intentionally avoided sockets and did not run a live HTTP server that returns a real 302 response into `_default_fetch`.
- The PoC did not demonstrate a specific sensitive file in a deployed worker image; impact depends on readable worker-local files, environment, injected credentials, and mounts.
- The PoC did not invoke the FastAPI artifact route; source and existing tests confirm declared artifacts are served by id.

## Uncertainty

The redirect and artifact path are strongly validated, but deployed severity depends on whether `UrlGrabEngine` is exposed to untrusted submitters and what sensitive files or environment variables are readable inside the worker. Confidence is below full end-to-end because the live HTTP redirect hop was not exercised in this sandbox.

## Next Step

Fix by enforcing the same `http`/`https` scheme policy after each redirect, or by using an opener restricted to HTTP(S) handlers only, then add a regression test for a redirect to `file:` that must fail before artifact creation.

## Artifact Paths

- Rubric: `/tmp/codex-security-scans/blastbox/c7e9f28495e1_20260626T144948Z/artifacts/05_findings/BB-FD-S0006-001/validation_artifacts/rubric.md`
- PoC script: `/tmp/codex-security-scans/blastbox/c7e9f28495e1_20260626T144948Z/artifacts/05_findings/BB-FD-S0006-001/validation_artifacts/poc_redirect_file_artifact.py`
- PoC log: `/tmp/codex-security-scans/blastbox/c7e9f28495e1_20260626T144948Z/artifacts/05_findings/BB-FD-S0006-001/validation_artifacts/poc_redirect_file_artifact.log`
- PoC result JSON: `/tmp/codex-security-scans/blastbox/c7e9f28495e1_20260626T144948Z/artifacts/05_findings/BB-FD-S0006-001/validation_artifacts/poc_0g9ehaa0/poc_result.json`
- PoC sealed body: `/tmp/codex-security-scans/blastbox/c7e9f28495e1_20260626T144948Z/artifacts/05_findings/BB-FD-S0006-001/validation_artifacts/poc_0g9ehaa0/out/body.bin`

## Closure

